 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per voxel, requiring 4 texture lookups (or function evaluations) [S2][S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per voxel. No additional storage for gradient vectors is required if computed on-the-fly [S5].
-*   **Performance:** Highly efficient for real-time rendering due to minimal memory accesses [S5]. However, the visual quality is inferior to central differences [S5].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, low memory footprint, suitable for dynamic deformations where gradients cannot be precomputed [S5].
-*   **Limitations:** First-order accuracy leads to blocky shading artifacts. Asymmetric stencil may cause directional bias in lighting [S1, unofficial][S5].
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time volume rendering with dynamic deformations, or when memory bandwidth is a critical bottleneck and visual artifacts can be mitigated by prefiltering [S5].
-*   **When Not to Use:** High-quality static volume rendering where smooth shading is required, or when precise gradient values are needed for segmentation [S3, unofficial][S5].
+**Time and Memory Complexity**
+- **Time**: $O(1)$ per voxel. It requires 4 texture lookups (current + 3 neighbors) per voxel [S3, S5, unofficial].
+- **Memory**: $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per voxel. No additional storage for gradient fields is required if computed on-the-fly [S6].
+
+**Measured Performance**
+- The method is very cheap to compute [S4, unofficial]. It is significantly faster than Sobel or Neumann gradients which require more samples and complex weights [S4, unofficial].
+- In deformed volumes, computing gradients on-the-fly in the fragment shader avoids precomputation but increases per-fragment cost [S6].
+
+**Scaling**
+- Scales linearly with the number of voxels.
+- For deformed volumes, the cost is independent of the deformation complexity if the deformation function is efficient [S6].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations**
+- **Strengths**: Minimal kernel size (4 voxels), very fast computation, detects high frequencies that central difference might lose [S4, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: First-order accuracy leads to blocky artifacts [S6]. Anisotropic and orientation-dependent [S4, unofficial]. Requires careful handling of boundaries. Poor gradient estimation without prefiltering [S6].
+
+**When to Use**
+- Use when computational resources are limited and real-time performance is critical [S4, unofficial].
+- Use when high-frequency detail preservation is more important than smoothness [S4, unofficial].
+- Use in deformed volumes where precomputed gradients are infeasible [S6].
+
+**When Not to Use**
+- Do not use when high accuracy and isotropy are required (use Central Difference or Sobel) [S4, unofficial].
+- Do not use if the data orientation is not fixed or known, as flipping axes changes the gradient [S4, unofficial].
+- Do not use without prefiltering if blocky artifacts are unacceptable [S6].
 
 ## Background
-The forward difference method is a fundamental numerical technique for approximating derivatives [S1, unofficial][S2]. In volume rendering, it was popularized in early direct volume rendering (DVR) implementations for lighting calculations, such as those described by Drebin et al. (1988) and in subsequent lecture notes [S4, unofficial][S5]. It is often referred to as the "intermediate difference gradient" in some literature, distinguishing it from central differences [S3, unofficial]. The method is particularly relevant in dynamic volume visualization, where on-the-fly gradient computation is necessary due to time-varying deformations [S5].
+The Forward Difference method is a fundamental numerical differentiation technique. In volume rendering, it was employed in early direct volume rendering implementations for lighting calculations. Drebin et al. (1988) and Sabella (1988) are foundational papers in volume rendering that discuss gradient-based lighting, though the specific forward difference implementation is often cited in later tutorials and reports as a simple baseline [S5, unofficial]. The method is described in various educational resources and unofficial reports as a standard first-order approximation [S1, S5, unofficial]. It is particularly relevant in contexts involving dynamic deformations, where gradients must be computed on-the-fly in fragment shaders, as demonstrated in EuroVis 2006 work [S6]. The inefficiency of finite differences for multivariate functions (requiring $N+1$ evaluations for $N$ variables) is a general property noted in differentiable rendering literature [S2].
 
